 To find the total surface area of the pyramid, we need to calculate the area of the base and the area of the four triangular faces. The base is a square with a side length of 10 cm, so its area is \(10 \text{ cm} \times 10 \text{ cm} = 100 \text{ cm}^2\). To find the area of the triangular faces, we first need to determine the slant height of the pyramid. The slant height is the hypotenuse of a right triangle formed by the height of the pyramid, half the side length of the base, and the slant height itself. The height of the pyramid is 12 cm, and half the side length of the base is \(10/2 = 5 \text{ cm}\). Using the Pythagorean theorem, the slant height \(l\) is given by:
\[
l = \sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} = \sqrt{144 + 25} = \sqrt{169} = 13 \text{ cm}
\]
